---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout id="specialized-units" title="Specialized Units">

  <section>
    <h2>Overview</h2>
    <p>
      Lunar Lake is built from two <strong>tiles</strong>. "Tile" is Intel's term for what the
      industry generally calls a chiplet: a separate piece of silicon (a die) that does one job,
      with several assembled into one package using advanced packaging. Instead of one big
      monolithic chip, the processor is made of smaller dies. The two tiles are the
      <strong>compute tile</strong> and the <strong>platform controller tile (PCT)</strong>, and
      the DRAM sits on the same package next to them.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Memory on package</h2>
    <p>
      The DRAM is placed on the package, right next to the tiles. The wires between the processor
      and the memory are therefore short, and interface power is much lower than with memory on
      the motherboard. The memory runs at up to 8.5 GT/s. The compute tile also contains the
      memory subsystem and a <strong>memory-side cache</strong>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>The two tiles</h2>

    <h3>Compute tile</h3>
    <p>
      The compute tile puts all of the processing units ("XPUs") together:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>4 Lion Cove P-cores</li>
      <li>4 Skymont E-cores</li>
      <li>NPU 4 architecture</li>
      <li>Xe2 GPU architecture</li>
      <li>Media and display engines</li>
      <li>Image processing unit</li>
      <li>The memory subsystem, including the memory-side cache</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The goal is to give these blocks the best process technology, so Intel gets the most out of
      the transistors, and to keep them close together. When they reside on the same die, latency
      between them stays low.
    </p>

    <h3>Platform controller tile (PCT)</h3>
    <p>
      The PCT handles I/O and platform functions. It divides into two major areas:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Security:</strong> three separate security engines, each specializing in different
        tasks.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Connectivity:</strong> integrated Wi-Fi 7, bluetooth 5.4 and Thunderbolt 4
    
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Why split the chip into tiles</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>
        The expensive leading-edge process is used only for the part that benefits from it.
        The PCT does not need cutting-edge transistors.
      </li>
      <li>Dies can be mixed and matched from different fabs.</li>
      <li>
        Yield improves, since small dies have fewer defects than one huge die.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <!-- verify: process node used for each tile -->
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Shared fabric and "extended scalability"</h2>
    <p>
      Both tiles are wired into the same on-chip network (the <strong>fabric</strong>), so a block
      connects to the system the same way on either die. That lets Intel decide which die each
      IP block goes on when designing a product, without redesigning how the block connects.
      Intel calls this <strong>extended scalability</strong>.
    </p>
    <p>
      An <strong>IP block</strong> is a reusable functional block, such as a USB controller, a PCIe
      controller, a memory controller, a media engine, or an NPU. Each is designed once and dropped
      into different chips. Because the fabric interface is identical on both tiles, a block does
      not care which die it sits on.
    </p>
    <p>
      This is a design-time choice when the chip is partitioned into tiles. Blocks do not move
      while the chip is running. Crossing between tiles still costs slightly more latency and
      energy than staying on one die, which is probably the reason the latency-sensitive blocks are kept
      together on the compute tile.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>GPU: Xe2</h2>
    <p>
      The integrated GPU uses Intel's second-generation Xe architecture, Xe2. It sits on the
      compute tile and shares the package and memory with the CPU cores. The media engine and
      display engine are part of the Xe2 architecture.
    </p>

    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Block</th>
          <th scope="col">What it does</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">XMX engines</th>
          <td>
            Xe Matrix eXtensions: dedicated hardware units for matrix multiplication, the core
            operation of AI and neural-network workloads. They perform many multiply-accumulates
            per cycle on low-precision numbers (INT8, FP16, BF16), much faster and at lower energy
            than the regular shader cores. This is Intel's counterpart to Nvidia's tensor cores,
            and it speeds up AI work on the GPU such as upscaling or running ML models.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Media engine</th>
          <td>
            A fixed-function block that encodes and decodes video (H.264, H.265, AV1, and so on).
            It is separate from the shader cores because dedicated hardware uses much less power,
            which is why video playback barely consumes the battery. It supports hardware
            <strong>H.266 (VVC)</strong> decoding. VVC is the successor to H.265 and compresses
            video considerably better. 
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Display engine</th>
          <td>
            Takes finished frames from memory and sends them to screens: scanning out pixels,
            scaling, color handling, and driving the output ports. It is separate from the
            rendering part of the GPU. It has <strong>three display pipelines</strong>, each an
            independent path generating the image stream for one display, so it can drive up to
            three displays at once with their own resolutions and timing.
          </td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>NPU 4</h2>
    <p>
      NPU 4 is the dedicated AI block on the compute tile. It has <strong>6 NPUs</strong>,
      <strong>12 SHAVE DSPs</strong>, and <strong>9 MB of cache</strong>.
      Its two kinds of hardware split the work of running a neural network:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Matrix engines (MAC arrays):</strong> hardwired units that do huge numbers of
        multiply-accumulates for the core math of a network, such as convolutions and matrix
        multiplies. Very fast and efficient, but fixed in function.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>SHAVE DSPs:</strong> flexible, programmable vector processors that handle
        everything else that does not map onto the matrix engines, such as activation functions,
        normalization, pooling, softmax, and data reshaping or type conversion.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Without the SHAVEs, any layer the matrix engines cannot handle would have to go back to the
      CPU or GPU, which costs time and energy. Because the SHAVEs are programmable, they also let
      the NPU support new kinds of layers through software updates, without changing the hardware.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Power management (PMIC)</h2>
    <p>
      The power management IC (PMIC) supplies the processor's power rails, each a dedicated supply
      line at its own voltage. Two features stand out.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>E-cores can be turned off.</strong> The E-cores can be power gated: when they are
        idle or not needed, their supply is cut entirely. A powered-off core uses almost no
        energy, not even leakage (the current transistors waste just by being powered, which is a
        big part of total power in modern processes). Gating a core is far better than letting it
        sit idle. The cache associated with the E-cores is turned off as well, while the P-cores
        keep working independently.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>E-cores have their own power rail.</strong> The PMIC outputs several rails, and one
        goes only to the E-cores. That gives them an independent voltage. It also gives
        independent on/off control: with a shared rail, cores can only be gated by switches inside
        the chip, while a separate rail lets the whole E-core group be shut down or ramped on its
        own.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The E-core rail is a <strong>fixed rail</strong>. The PMIC also provides
      <strong>variable rails</strong>; the DRAM rail is one example.
    </p>
    <p>
      The PMIC also provides <strong>telemetry</strong>: it reports back how much power each rail
      is delivering, so the processor knows its real consumption.
    </p>
  </section>

</Layout>

